## TIẾNG ANH · 16:30–17:00 — Thứ Năm: Hỏi đáp phỏng vấn

**Ba câu, mỗi câu 60 giây, ghi âm liền mạch không dừng:**

**Q1. "How do you make sure your app works for everyone?"**

> *"Three things I actually do, not aspirations.*
> *First, I test with the system font size turned all the way up and with dark mode on — those two catch most layout problems.*
> *Second, I write component tests using accessibility labels as the selector. That's a testing choice, but it forces me to label things properly, so the labels are always there and always correct.*
> *Third, any hidden gesture has a visible alternative. Swipe to delete is nice, but a screen reader user can't do it, so long press opens a menu that does the same thing."*

**Q2. "What's the difference between a loading state and a skeleton?"**

> *"A spinner tells you the app is busy. A skeleton tells you what's coming and roughly how much of it.*
> *I use skeletons where the layout is predictable — a list of meal rows, for example — because the screen doesn't jump when the data lands. I use a spinner for things where I genuinely don't know the shape yet, like an AI response.*
> *The thing I'd avoid is a full-screen spinner over content that's already loaded. If I'm refreshing a list I already have, I keep the list visible and show the refresh indicator at the top."*

**Q3. "Tell me about a bug you found in your own app."**

> *"I did a full walkthrough of the app yesterday from a fresh install, and I found that double-tapping the save button quickly creates two meals.*
> *The cause is that I only guard against it in the mutation's onSuccess, which runs too late — the second tap fires before the first request resolves. The fix is to disable the button on press using the mutation's pending state.*
> *What I took from it: I never would have found that in normal use, because I know how the app works and I don't tap twice. The value of walking through as a stranger is that you do the things a stranger does."*

